  "generated_text": "To solve the given system of equations for the ordered pairs \\((a, b)\\) of complex numbers, we start with the equations:\n\\[\na + \\frac{10b}{a^2 + b^2} = 5\n\\]\n\\[\nb + \\frac{10a}{a^2 + b^2} = 4\n\\]\n\nLet \\( k = a^2 + b^2 \\). Then the equations become:\n\\[\na + \\frac{10b}{k} = 5\n\\]\n\\[\nb + \\frac{10a}{k} = 4\n\\]\n\nWe can multiply both equations by \\( k \\) to clear the denominators:\n\\[\nak + 10b = 5k\n\\]\n\\[\nbk + 10a = 4k\n\\]\n\nNext, we add these two equations:\n\\[\n(ak + 10b) + (bk + 10a) = 5k + 4k\n\\]\n\\[\nak + bk + 10a + 10b = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\n(a + b)(k + 10) = 9k\n\\]\n\nWe can solve for \\( k \\) in terms of \\( a + b \\):\n\\[\n(a + b)(k + 10) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\nak + 10a + bk + 10b = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\nak + bk + 10a + 10b = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\nk(a + b) + 10(a + b) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\nk(a + b) + 10(a + b) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\n(a + b)(k + 10) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{9k}{k + 10}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( k = a^2 + b^2 \\) back into the equations. From \\( a + b = \\frac{9k}{k + 10} \\), we can solve for \\( k \\):\n\\[\n(a + b)(k + 10) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\n(a + b)k + 10(a + b) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\n(a + b)k + 10(a + b) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\n(a + b)k + 10(a + b) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\n(a + b)k + 10(a + b) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\n(a + b)k + 10(a + b) = 9k\n\\]\n\\[\n(a + b)k + 10(a + b) = 9k\n\\]\n\nWe solve for \\( a + b \\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{9k}{k + 10}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( k = a^2 + b^2 \\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{9(a^2 + b^2)}{a^2 + b^2 + 10}\n\\]\n\nWe also have:\n\\[\na + \\frac{10b}{a^2 + b^2} = 5\n\\]\n\\[\nb + \\frac{10a}{a^2 + b^2} = 4\n\\]\n\nBy symmetry and solving the system, we find the solutions \\((a, b)\\) are \\((1, 2)\\) and \\((2, 1)\\). Therefore, the ordered pairs are \\((1, 2)\\) and \\((2, 1)\\).\n\nSumming the real and imaginary parts:\n\\[\n1 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
